    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Is the Sea in Madeira Clean Enough to Swim In?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>27 November 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Yes, for the most part. Most of Madeira&#x27;s 60 official bathing waters were rated excellent in the EU&#x27;s 2025 assessment, and the open coast is washed by deep Atlantic water, but two sites were rated poor, and any beach near a stream mouth or a harbour is best skipped for a day or two after heavy rain.</p>

      <h2>How is sea water quality checked in Madeira?</h2><p>By laboratory tests during the bathing season. Under EU rules, each official bathing water is sampled for two kinds of bacteria that come from sewage and animal waste, <em>E. coli</em> and intestinal enterococci. The results from recent seasons give each site a yearly grade: excellent, good, sufficient or poor. Madeira had 60 official bathing waters in 2025 and 61 in 2024.</p><p>Across Portugal, 559 of 682 bathing waters, or 82%, were rated excellent for the 2025 season, according to the European Environment Agency's report of June 2026. In Madeira most sites were excellent in both 2024 and 2025. In the 2024 classification, every Madeiran bathing water that was not excellent was rated good or poor, and only two were poor. Seventeen Madeiran beaches also flew a Blue Flag in 2026, an award that requires excellent water.</p><h2>Which places have had problems?</h2><p>A handful, and they are worth knowing. In the 2025 classification two Madeiran bathing waters were rated poor:</p><ul><li><strong>Poças do Gomes at Doca do Cavacas</strong>, the lava rock pools in western Funchal</li><li><strong>Quinta do Lorde</strong>, near Caniçal at the east end of the island, which was closed to swimmers more than once in 2025 after contamination was found</li></ul><p>A poor grade does not mean the water is dirty every day. It means too many samples over recent seasons had high bacteria counts. Sufficient is the minimum the EU expects, and below it the health authority can advise against swimming or ban it.</p><p>In the 2024 classification the two poor sites were Doca do Cavacas and Praia do Vigário in Câmara de Lobos. Temporary bans happen too. The regional health authority closed Praia do Vigário to swimming on 20 May 2026 after contamination was detected, and lifted the ban two days later once new tests came back clear. Praia da Maiata in Machico was closed in June 2025 for the same reason.</p><h2>Why is the open coast so clean?</h2><p>Because the water around Madeira is deep, always moving and low in nutrients. The island rises steeply from the ocean floor, so the seabed falls away fast. Swell and currents keep the water changing. There are no large rivers, no wide farmland plains and little heavy industry draining into the sea. Those low nutrient levels keep the water clear, and they are also why it looks so blue: see <a href="/posts/why-is-the-sea-around-madeira-so-blue">why the sea around Madeira is so blue</a>.</p><p>Problems, when they come, come from the land: a sewage overflow, a storm drain, a stream carrying the run-off from a town. That is why the poor results in recent years have been at pools, small harbours and town beaches, not along open cliffs.</p><h2>What changes after heavy rain?</h2><p>A lot, for a short time. Madeira's streams, the <em>ribeiras</em>, run low most of the year and turn brown and fast after a storm. They carry soil, leaves, road run-off and sometimes overflowing drains straight into the sea. Funchal alone has three, the São João, Santa Luzia and João Gomes, all reaching the sea along the city's seafront. Portugal's environment agency names stormwater drainage during heavy rain as a known source of contamination for bathing waters.</p><p>You can often see it from a boat: a brown plume spreading out from a stream mouth into the blue. After a heavy downpour, give it a day or two before swimming near a town beach, a harbour or any stream mouth, even if no official ban has been posted. Along the cliffs, away from any outflow, the water is usually clean again sooner. Heavy rain is far more common from October to March, so this matters most in winter.</p><h2>Is it safe to swim from a boat off the cliffs?</h2><p>Usually, yes, with one caveat: open-water swim spots are often not official bathing waters, so nobody takes lab samples there. What protects you is distance from outflows and plain common sense. Chifbay's <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a> swims in open water, below the cliffs at Fajã dos Padres and later off Ribeira Brava, and the skipper picks the exact place when you get there. Rough days are covered on our <a href="/practical">practical information</a> page, including what happens to your booking.</p><p>It depends on the day, though. After a big winter storm, a town beach like Ribeira Brava, which sits at the mouth of its own stream, may not be the place to swim for a while, and a sensible skipper looks elsewhere. Nobody should feel pushed into the water. For the other worry swimmers raise, see <a href="/posts/jellyfish-in-madeira-is-it-safe-to-swim">whether jellyfish make Madeira unsafe for swimming</a>.</p><h2>How do you check before you swim?</h2><p>Five quick checks cover almost everything:</p><ul><li>Look up the beach on the Praias de Portugal website run by APA, the Portuguese Environment Agency, which shows the grade of every official bathing water.</li><li>Check the regional health authority's notices on madeira.gov.pt for temporary bans.</li><li>At staffed beaches and lidos, read the flags. A red flag means no swimming, often because of waves rather than water quality.</li><li>Skip any spot with brown water, a floating scum line or a bad smell, whatever its grade says.</li><li>Stay away from harbour mouths and stream mouths after rain.</li></ul><p>Most days, none of this will stop you. On a Chifbay trip the call on the sea is the skipper's, but these checks are yours for any swim you take on your own during your stay.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is Funchal's sea clean enough to swim in?</h3><p>Mostly, yes. Doca do Cavacas in western Funchal was rated poor in 2024 and 2025, but in the 2024 classification every other Madeiran bathing water was rated excellent or good.</p><h3>How long after rain should you wait to swim in Madeira?</h3><p>A day or two near town beaches, harbours and stream mouths after heavy rain. The open coast away from any outflow clears sooner.</p><h3>Who decides when a beach in Madeira is closed?</h3><p>The regional health authority issues temporary bans after bad lab results and lifts them when new tests are clear. Red flags for waves are a separate decision made at staffed beaches.</p><h3>Are the natural pools in Madeira clean?</h3><p>It varies by site. The Poças do Gomes pools at Doca do Cavacas were rated poor in 2025, while Funchal's bathing complexes again won Blue Flags in 2026. Check each one before you go.</p>

      <blockquote>The sea here is clean because the island gives it so little. Watch the days when it gives too much.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

